// anchor: unproven-callee-targets
// diff-backends: vm
// diff-aot-reject: call target has no consumable adapter authority
// AOT native plans every indirect call by proving a finite set of target
// functions for it. The proof itself moved: the semantic plan now states a
// call target for these shapes, and the target layer refuses because it
// consumes only some target kinds (DIRECT_LOCAL, SOURCE_EXPORT,
// NATIVE_NAMESPACE_YIELDABLE, BUILTIN_INSTANCE_YIELDABLE and
// SOURCE_CLASS_CONSTRUCTOR). So the refusal no longer says the target could
// not be proven -- it says nothing consumes the kind that was proven:
//   XR_TARGET_1003: call target has no consumable adapter authority
// The VM runs all of it.
//
// Three provenances reach the same wall, and assigning to a local first does
// not help -- the planner traces through SSA, so the local is just a COPY:
//   * a function value captured as an upvalue
//   * a function value read out of a Map
//   * a function value read out of an array the loop built
//
// These shapes are proven and do compile, which is what makes the boundary
// worth pinning: a callback parameter, a local initialised from a lambda, a
// class field, a returned lambda, and an array whose elements are literals.
//
// The actionable diagnostic this contract exists to protect -- the one naming
// the provenance and suggesting a rewrite -- lives in the AOT callable layer
// and is no longer reached, because the target layer refuses first. Restoring
// it means carrying provenance into the target layer's refusal, not restoring
// the old wording here.
//
// The closed-world planner is deliberate, so the resolution is a real user
// diagnostic plus a spec statement of the limit, not making these compile: the
// AOT backend rejects each shape with an actionable message that names the
// provenance and suggests a rewrite, while the VM runs the program. This case
// checks the VM run; the native rejection is a compile-time diagnostic and is
// covered as the symmetric AOT rejection half of this differential contract.
type Mapper = fn(int) -> int
type IntThunk = fn() -> int

fn double(x: int) -> int { return x * 2 }
fn negate(x: int) -> int { return 0 - x }

fn compose(f: Mapper, g: Mapper) -> Mapper { return (x) -> g(f(x)) }

fn main() {
    var dn = compose(double, negate)
    print(dn(5))                     // -10 -- callee is an upvalue

    var table = Map<string, Mapper>()
    table.set("dbl", double)
    print(table.get("dbl")!(7))      // 14 -- callee comes out of a Map

    var fs: Array<IntThunk> = []
    for (i in 0..3) { fs.push(() -> i) }
    print(fs[0]())                   // 0 -- array built by a loop
    print(fs[2]())                   // 2
}

main()
